  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1cdb4ee1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Recommended Layer for Flood Concept Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "**Best overall layer for interpretable flood concepts:**  \n",
    "`layer4.1.conv2`\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why this layer?\n",
    "- Positioned in the late-backbone *stage-4 sweet spot*: features are class-biased but still spatially sharp.  \n",
    "- Full CRP dumps (`*_data.npy`, `*_rel.npy`, `*_rf.npy`) and normalized Act/Rel stats are available for this layer.  \n",
    "- Concepts are clear, local, and map well to flood textures and shorelines.  \n",
    "\n",
    "**Compared to neighbors:**\n",
    "- **Earlier blocks (`conv1.*`, `layer2.*`):** More noise, less semantic clarity.  \n",
    "- **Deeper/head layers (`layer5.*`, `pag*`, `spp*`, `final_layer.*`):** Capture more global context, but concepts become diffuse and less interpretable.  \n",
    "\n",
    "**Backup choice:**  \n",
    "`layer4.2.conv2` — slightly deeper; sometimes more semantic, but often blurrier.  \n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## How \"Concept Goodness\" Was Judged\n",
    "\n",
    "- **ActMax / RelStats:** Used to gauge saliency strength and consistency.  \n",
    "- **Per-layer CRP files:** Inspected for locality, coverage, and redundancy (both visually and numerically).  \n",
    "\n",
    "**Winning behavior for `layer4.1.conv2`:**\n",
    "- **Purity:** Clusters map cleanly to flood textures and shorelines.  \n",
    "- **Locality:** Receptive fields capture water regions without smearing relevance.  \n",
    "- **Coverage:** Multiple distinct prototypes (e.g., ripples, reflective surfaces, muddy boundaries).  \n",
    "- **Low redundancy:** Fewer duplicate or overlapping concepts.  \n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Pros & Cons by Region\n",
    "\n",
    "### Stem & Early Backbone (`conv1.0`, `conv1.3`, `layer1.*`)\n",
    "- **Pros:** Excellent edges and textures, stable.  \n",
    "- **Cons:** Weak class bias, concepts appear generic.  \n",
    "\n",
    "### Mid Backbone (`layer2.*`, `layer3.*`)\n",
    "- **Pros:** Capture more structure (e.g., banks, vegetation vs. water).  \n",
    "- **Cons:** Still mixed; sensitive to illumination and patterns.  \n",
    "\n",
    "### Late Backbone (Recommended, `layer4.*`)\n",
    "- **Pros:** Best trade-off; class-biased and local; strong concept clarity.  \n",
    "- **Cons:** Slightly more compute; less global context than `layer5` or heads.  \n",
    "\n",
    "### Very Late / Decoder-like Blocks (`layer5.*`, `pag*`, `spp*`)\n",
    "- **Pros:** Provide global context and scene-level cues.  \n",
    "- **Cons:** Concepts become diffuse, with a higher risk of class-mixing.  \n",
    "\n",
    "### Head / Logits Prep (`final_layer.conv1/conv2`, `segmentation_head.0`)\n",
    "- **Pros:** Directly tied to predictions.  \n",
    "- **Cons:** Lowest interpretability at the concept level.  \n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## **Summary**\n",
    "For flood concept analysis, **use `layer4.1.conv2`** to achieve the best balance between semantic clarity and spatial locality.  \n",
    "`layer4.2.conv2` is a viable fallback, but tends to produce slightly blurrier concepts.\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
